    },
    {
      "id": "e29d2d8a-6616-4423-82a1-38a9dfb90446",
      "phaseId": "1b6f739f-8774-4da2-af4f-5f88d0514507",
      "number": 387,
      "shortId": "GM7M6",
      "priority": 5,
      "shortName": "label-marks-matched-characters",
      "title": "Label marks matched characters — the last thing about text the palette hand-draws",
      "status": "done",
      "description": "The prerequisite for the palette's rows becoming composed widgets. Without it a composed `Label` cannot show a fuzzy match, and the palette has to keep painting its own text.\n\n## What the palette does today\n\n`heca-grid-ui/src/widgets/command_palette.rs:688-714` — it draws the row's label, then **over-draws each matched character in the accent colour**, positioning each by index against the monospace cell:\n\n```\n// Label, then over-draw matched chars in accent.\nfor &hi in &m.hits { … }\n```\n\n`Match.hits` (`:90`) is the list of matched char indices the fuzzy matcher returns. This is the third capability the palette hand-rolled because `Label` lacked it — truncation was the first (T379), wrapping the second (T382).\n\n## What to build\n\n`Label::marks(Vec<usize>)` + `Label::mark_color(Color)` (or one `marks(indices, color)` — pick one and say why): the listed **character indices** are drawn in the mark colour, the rest in the label's resolved colour.\n\nGeneric on purpose: any search surface highlights matches, and the indices come from `search::fuzzy` (P092/T384), which returns them for exactly this. Nothing here knows what a command is.\n\n## Where it interacts\n\n- **Truncation** (`drawn_text`, `:124`): the drawn string is a *cut* of the original, so an index into the original text is not an index into the drawn one. `Ellipsis::End` keeps a prefix (indices survive, past the cut are dropped); `Ellipsis::Start` drops `len - n + 1` from the head (every surviving index shifts). Get this wrong and the highlight lands on the wrong letters — visible, and exactly the kind of thing a test must assert on the drawn output.\n- **Wrapping** (T382, `drawn_lines`): an index must be mapped to (line, column). `wrap_lines` collapses runs of whitespace, so the mapping cannot be recovered by counting characters in the output — either return the source offsets from the wrap, or mark before wrapping. Decide and document.\n- **Decorations** (`run_rects`): unaffected — a mark is a colour, not a rect.\n\n## Edge cases\n\n- An index past the end of the text is ignored, not a panic — the caller's indices and the label's text can disagree by a frame when a signal updates.\n- Multi-byte text: indices are **chars**, never bytes, like every other measure in this widget.\n- No marks ⇒ one text run exactly as today. This must not split the draw into per-character runs for ordinary labels.\n\n## Test\n\nPaint and read the `DrawCommand::Text` runs: the marked characters come out in the mark colour and the rest in the label colour, with the concatenation still equal to the drawn text. With `truncate(Start)` the marks follow the characters they were attached to, not their original positions. Unmarked labels still paint exactly one run.\n\n---\n**Completion summary:**\nDone. `Label::marks(indices)` + `Label::mark_color(color)` in heca-grid-ui/src/widgets/label.rs; SDK setter for `mark_color` in heca-view/src/build.rs (the drift guard demanded it, as it did for `truncate` and `wrap`). `marks` is `host_only` — a list, not a scalar.\n\n**The mechanism that makes it correct: `drawn_indexed()`.** The cut and the reflow are applied in one place, and every drawn character carries its index in the **source** text (`None` for the ellipsis, which the label added itself). Marks are then a lookup on that index, so they survive both transformations by construction rather than by arithmetic: `Ellipsis::Start` shifts every surviving character, and wrapping collapses whitespace, so counting through the drawn output would drift. `drawn_lines()` is now derived from it, so there is one implementation of the cut.\n\n`font::wrap_indexed()` is the same generalisation one level down: it returns each character's source index, and `wrap_lines()` is derived from it. The joining space carries the index of the whitespace it stands for.\n\nPaint draws runs of same-markedness, each placed from the line's aligned origin (`run_rects`) at `TextAlign::Start`. **An unmarked label still paints exactly one run** — asserted, because splitting every label into pieces would cost the whole tree for a feature almost nothing uses. Marks are drawn bold + accent, matching what the palette hand-painted at command_palette.rs:714-723.\n\nTests assert the drawn runs and their colours: the pieces still spell the text; exactly the marked characters are accented; `Ellipsis::Start` carries the marks with the characters it kept; `Ellipsis::End` marks nothing once those characters are cut (and stamps nothing onto the ellipsis); a wrapped label marks the right word on the line it wrapped to. One test index was mine to fix — \"lazy\" starts at 35, not 34; the code had marked exactly what was asked for.\n\nNot visually verified: nothing consumes it yet. That arrives with T388.",
      "notes": "",
