    {
      "id": "7fbccecd-37e4-4023-999e-85cc1b42acc0",
      "phaseId": "1b6f739f-8774-4da2-af4f-5f88d0514507",
      "number": 388,
      "shortId": "2JDWW",
      "priority": 6,
      "shortName": "the-palette-s-rows-become-comp",
      "title": "The palette's rows become composed children — Select's pattern, so wrap/truncate/marks come from the widgets",
      "status": "planned",
      "description": "Stop the palette painting text. Its rows become real child components built from `Label`, the icon widgets and the keycap chips, so truncation (T379), wrapping (T382) and match marks (T387) arrive as **properties** instead of being re-implemented in `paint`.\n\nDepends on T387 (a composed label cannot show a match without it).\n\n## The precedent — this is a solved problem in this crate\n\n`Select` is an overlay panel whose rows are **real children** and it works today:\n- Options are pushed as children: `heca-grid-ui/src/widgets/select.rs:188` (`self.base.children.push(Box::new(choice))`).\n- The engine lays them out in the control's own flow and **measures** them there; `on_layout` (`:554`) captures each row's measured height.\n- `place_options` (`:365`) then moves each visible row into the overlay panel by baking an **absolute** target into its bounds and calling `shift_subtree` for descendants — idempotent, so re-running never compounds an offset. Rows outside the visible window collapse to zero size so no stale rect swallows a click.\n- Why it must be done that way is written at `select.rs:21-26`: \"the layout engine cannot place it there — a taffy node sits where its parent puts it\".\n\nThe palette needs exactly this, and currently does none of it: `routes_own_subtree()` returns true and its rows are \"drawn from data, not mounted as children\".\n\n## What the palette hand-draws today (`command_palette.rs`, ~957 lines)\n\n`paint` (`:557`) draws: the panel chrome, the selected-row fill and left accent bar, the icon, the label, the **match marks over-drawn per character** (`:688-714`), the description, and the keycap chips. Geometry is arithmetic: `row_h` (`:378`), `row_lines` (`:372`), `row_rects` (`:386`), `visible_rows` (`:325`), `layout` (`:445`).\n\n## What to build\n\n1. **A row child** composed of the existing widgets: the icon, a title `Label` (`marks` + `truncate(End)`), a description `Label` (`wrap(true)`), and the keycap stack. Built inside `heca-grid-ui` from generic widgets — a widget composing widgets, as `Select` composes `Choice`. It binds no app concept, so it is **not** an F011 component.\n2. **Measured heights replace the arithmetic.** `row_h`/`row_lines` go away; the height of a row is what the engine measured, captured in `on_layout` like `Select`'s. This is what makes the reflow work: a wrapping description reports its own height, so the rows below shift with no palette code for it.\n3. **`place_rows`** mirroring `place_options`: visible rows shifted to absolute targets inside the panel rect, off-window rows collapsed to zero.\n4. `visible_rows` and `layout` read the measured heights instead of computing them. **Keep the bottom margin** (`BOTTOM_FRAC`, T383) — it is a property of the panel, not of the rows.\n\n## Behaviours to preserve\n\n- The query line stays the hand-driven `Input` it is (`:181`) — that is a separate seam and not in scope.\n- The panel's own placement, size variants (`panel_metrics`, `:124`) and the bottom margin are unchanged.\n- Hit-testing: today paint, hover-select and the click hit-test all read one `row_rects`, which is what keeps a row from being drawn in one place and clicked in another. With real children the bounds **are** that single source — do not add a second geometry path beside them.\n- Rows outside the visible window must not be clickable (Select's zero-collapse).\n- The description-less list stays one line per row.\n\n## Edge cases\n\n- The palette is rebuilt on every open (`heca/src/chrome/palette.rs:185`), so children are built once per open — no incremental child management, but the row count changes as the **query filters**, which `Select` never has to handle. Decide whether rows are rebuilt per keystroke or built once for all commands and hidden — hiding is closer to Select and avoids rebuilding a hundred widgets per character.\n- A wrapping description makes rows different heights, so the running sum in `row_rects` must come from measured heights, not one height times an index.\n- First frame: `Select` notes its measured heights come from the previous layout pass (`:532`) and falls back to a font-derived height. The palette opens and paints immediately, so it needs the same fallback or the first frame is wrong.\n\n## Test\n\n- The drawn output is unchanged for a list without descriptions: same rows, same texts, same geometry as before the refactor.\n- Selecting a row whose description wraps makes the rows below it move down (assert their painted y), and the panel grows — the reflow, measured.\n- A click at a row's painted rect still runs that row's command; a click where a collapsed off-window row used to be runs nothing.\n- The match marks still land on the matched characters (now via `Label::marks`).\n\n**Visual verification is the user's** — this changes how every palette row is drawn.",
